{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Acute-onset dyspnea is one of the most common symptoms for which patients visit the Emergency Department (ED) [1-4]. In the United States, dyspnea is the main reason for four to five million ED visits annually [4], representing up to 50% of patients admitted to acute tertiary care hospitals [5]. In the Asia-Pacific region, 5% of all ED presentations are due to dyspnea [6]. In addition to its high incidence, the 30-day mortality rate of these patients remains relatively high (8-13%) [7, 8]. Therefore, rapid and appropriate diagnosis of the underlying pathology is of utmost importance for prompt and adequate treatment [9]. However, differential diagnosis is often challenging [10, 11]. Most physicians mainly rely on conventional diagnostic modalities, such as medical history, physical examination, chest X-ray (CXR), electrocardiogram (ECG), and standard laboratory tests [12]. Even given all these tests, some studies have raised doubts about the diagnostic accuracy of these conventional approaches, especially in the critically ill patient population [13, 14].",
      "reason": "The text describes the clinical context (acute dyspnea), the high incidence and mortality, the challenges in differential diagnosis, and the limitations of current conventional diagnostic approaches."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Therefore, we conducted a high-quality, comprehensive systematic review and meta-analysis that included the most recent publications that reported clinical outcomes with the use of PoCUS in patients who developed acute onset dyspnea. In addition to the existing diagnostic accuracy studies [16-20, 25], our main objective, as a new insight to this field, was to investigate how PoCUS improves clinical endpoints in patients with acute onset dyspnea.",
      "reason": "The authors explain the need for the review by stating their objective to investigate how PoCUS improves clinical endpoints, noting this as a new insight compared to existing diagnostic accuracy studies."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_19",
      "quote": "Garthlehner et al. [63] found no statistically significant differences for in-hospital mortality based on the analysis of three RCTs [39-41]. Since this review, two further studies have been published [42, 50] that were included in our analysis, thereby we found a tendency toward PoCUS reducing in-hospital mortality, but it was not significant.",
      "reason": "The authors identify and cite a previous review (Garthlehner et al.) and explain the need for the current review by noting that new studies have been published since that review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "The use of Point-of-care ultrasound (PoCUS) has gained increasing popularity in several domains of acute patient management, including acute onset dyspnea [11, 15]. There is an increasing body of evidence demonstrating that the accuracy of PoCUS is comparable to the current imaging reference standard CXR in general [16] as well as in specific conditions... PoCUS has other advantages, such as being free from ionizing radiation, and most importantly can be performed in real-time at the bedside [16, 21]. Additionally, PoCUS can answer a broad spectrum of remaining diagnostic questions and may also help to optimize and personalize therapy [22].",
      "reason": "The text describes the mechanism of action/utility of PoCUS (real-time bedside imaging, no ionizing radiation, ability to answer diagnostic questions and optimize therapy) in the context of the intervention being reviewed."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review does not focus on a complex intervention or delivery context requiring a logic model; it is a standard systematic review of a diagnostic/clinical intervention (PoCUS)."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "In addition to the existing diagnostic accuracy studies [16-20, 25], our main objective, as a new insight to this field, was to investigate how PoCUS improves clinical endpoints in patients with acute onset dyspnea.",
      "reason": "The review explicitly states its main objective in the introduction, and the study design is structured around a PICO framework as detailed in the methods section."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The selected studies had to match our previously defined PICO (Patients, Intervention, Control, Outcome) framework: ... P: Adults and children who were admitted to the ED or to the Intensive Care Unit (ICU), or to another inpatient setting because of acute onset or worsening dyspnea were eligible. ... I: The examined intervention was PoCUS use on its own or in combination with conventional diagnostic measures. ... C: Control group included conventional diagnostic methods, such as taking the patients' medical history, physical examination, ECG, blood gas and different laboratory analyses, echocardiography, CXR, or computer tomography (CT).",
      "reason": "The review explicitly defines the planned comparisons using the PICO framework, detailing the population, intervention, control, and outcomes."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "P: Adults and children who were admitted to the ED or to the Intensive Care Unit (ICU), or to another inpatient setting because of acute onset or worsening dyspnea were eligible. We also included studies enrolling patients who developed shortness of breath from unknown etiologies and were already hospitalized. Studies reporting on trauma-induced acute onset dyspnea, or pregnancy were excluded. I: The examined intervention was PoCUS use on its own or in combination with conventional diagnostic measures. C: Control group included conventional diagnostic methods, such as taking the patients' medical history, physical examination, ECG, blood gas and different laboratory analyses, echocardiography, CXR, or computer tomography (CT).",
      "reason": "The review explicitly defines the PICO (Patients, Intervention, Control, Outcome) framework and study designs (RCTs, prospective and retrospective cohort studies in text_6) used to determine eligibility."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "We did not use any restrictions or limitations based on language or publication date. We also scanned the reference lists of included studies and the cited articles in Google Scholar.",
      "reason": "The review specifies that there were no restrictions on language or publication date, and text_6 specifies the exclusion of editorials, review articles, case reports, case series, conference abstracts, non-peer-reviewed articles, and animal experiments."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review does not state whether studies were excluded because outcomes were not measured versus because outcome results were not reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_8",
      "quote": "If there were at least three studies for an outcome, a metaanalysis was performed, and the results displayed in forest plots. For continuous outcomes, pooled mean differences (MDs), and for dichotomous variables, pooled odds ratios (ORs) along with their 95% confidence interval (CI), were calculated to investigate the differences between the compared arms.",
      "reason": "The review specifies the synthesis method (meta-analysis using random effects model) and links the comparison (PoCUS vs. conventional diagnostic methods) to the review objectives."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "While the review lists exclusions (e.g., trauma-induced dyspnea, pregnancy, editorials), it does not provide a rationale for these specific restrictions."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

